+messages contained within them.  See <eref target="https://github.com/RunxiaWan/PcapParser" brackets="angle"/>.</t>
       <t indent="0" pn="section-appendix.d-4">DNS-layer-fragmentation implements DNS proxies that perform
 application-level fragmentation of DNS messages, based on
 <xref target="FRAGMENTS" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="FRAGMENTS"/>.  The idea with these proxies is to explore splitting DNS
 messages in the protocol itself, so they will not by fragmented by
-the IP layer.  See &lt;https://github.com/BII-Lab/DNS-layer-
-Fragmentation&gt;.</t>
+the IP layer.  See <eref target="https://github.com/BII-Lab/DNS-layer-Fragmentation" brackets="angle"/>.</t>
       <t indent="0" pn="section-appendix.d-5">DNS_ATR is an implementation of DNS Additional Truncated Response
 (ATR), as described in <xref target="ATR" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="ATR"/> and <xref target="HOW_ATR_WORKS" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="HOW_ATR_WORKS"/>.  DNS_ATR acts as a
 proxy between resolver and authoritative servers, forwarding queries
 and responses as a silent and transparent listener.  Responses that
 are larger than a nominated threshold (1280 octets by default)
 trigger additional truncated responses to be sent immediately
-following the large response.  See &lt;https://github.com/songlinjian/
-DNS_ATR&gt;.</t>
+following the large response.  See <eref target="https://github.com/songlinjian/DNS_ATR" brackets="angle"/>.</t>
